


@@BEGIN: PRESENTATION
@id: washino
@title: Mazurka Performance Analysis with Sonic Visualiser: An Approach Utilizing Heterogeneous Recordings
@presenter: washino
@abstract: 

This paper proposes a performance analysis methodology for
audio recordings and piano rolls utilizing Sonic
Visualiser. While each of these historical recording media
possesses distinct advantages and disadvantages, an
analytical approach that accounts for their respective
characteristics allows researchers to maximize the utility
of a highly limited corpus of historical materials.
Crucially, using the same software, Sonic Visualiser,
enables the analysis of both media under identical
parameters, thereby facilitating a direct and reliable
comparison.

This study utilizes performance materials from the first
half of the twentieth century to analyze how the mazurka
was historically performed. Audio recording technology
emerged at the end of the nineteenth century, initially
capturing performances on cylindrical wax recordings
(cylinder recordings) before transitioning to SP recordings
(78-rpm records). However, both formats were constrained by
a maximum recording duration of approximately three to five
minutes. Furthermore, audio recordings are characterized by
inherent noise, and because all sounds are captured as a
single, combined waveform, it is difficult to isolate and
analyze how individual notes were produced.

Conversely, piano rolls allow for analysis free from the
interference of surface noise and provide both visual and
auditory accessibility. Moreover, the onset and offset
timings of each note are clearly discernible. Nevertheless,
caution is required, as this onset and offset information
may have been subject to subsequent editing. Regarding
dynamics (volume), the acoustic intensity was not directly
recorded from the live performance in the first place;
thus, even if the rolls reflect the aesthetic preferences
of the era, they cannot be considered strictly authentic in
terms of volume.

Despite the seemingly fatal flaws inherent in both types of
performance materials, the charm and historical value of
the recorded performances cannot be ignored. Given the
scarce number of surviving source materials compared to the
present day, it is essential to analyze these historical
recordings by leveraging and complementary balancing the
strengths of both media. Specifically, this study proposes
a comparative analysis based on three key premises: the
minimal editing found in audio recordings, the precise
onset positions provided by piano rolls, and the tendency
for identical repertoire performed by the same artist to
exhibit structural similarities.

Sonic Visualiser serves as an exceptionally powerful tool
for this comparative analysis. Not only can both media be
analyzed within this software, but they can also be
displayed side-by-side. For instance, when an audio
recording and a piano roll of the same piece by the same
pianist exist, Sonic Visualiser can detect onset timings
and visually align the two sources, illustrating how
rhythms were articulated and how expressive phrasing was
shaped. Quantifying these detected timings—for example,
by calculating the Inter-Onset Intervals (IOI) of
individual notes in a triple-meter accompaniment—provides
substantial clues for analyzing rhythmic tendencies within
specific musical sections. Furthermore, even if a piano
roll sounds universally too fast or too slow despite being
the same pianist, such discrepancy often stems from an
incorrect playback speed of the roll. If the acceleration
or deceleration is proportional throughout the entire
performance, the correct playback speed can be
mathematically derived through calculation.

By presenting concrete case studies of audio and piano roll
analyses using Sonic Visualiser, this paper aims to
demonstrate the potential of this methodology for
performance analysis while simultaneously addressing
critical caveats that researchers must consider.



